#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "3d5ffe46-c0ac-5309-a31b-28a6a1d927e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9054" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/bff904851d01e984d3b1719623d6e4aa4fbde9f070cb4e3fa4121f96a55a34bf.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_1 \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-4I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/c7bc0b1331127f20b8121b32111c152029c75bd0c6b7ebb0abab5425445285f1.png)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/a1d998bfdf9ba31174396de548e12e0fae07fdbfa5c2a0e62b0c90ba8071ba64.png)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-4I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/bcf76f04db85e4207c2b26e2a7848812c631330f3d65129e93430df1e71592f6.png)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/17eb9f4a4768401d60cdd40aad65bb184799d5c718e1ff97a2204f48a0a8b46b.png)\n\nHere, we see that $A-4I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "30daf182-0ed6-5ddd-994e-a5af7c6c6c01" :question/math-academy-id "e-9056" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-5I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+3I$ and $A-5I$ on the nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v},\\mathbf{w},\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-3$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=5$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v}_n$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+3I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}2$ steps, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank ${\\color{red}2},$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{v} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{w} \\xrightarrow{A+{\\color{blue}3}I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+{\\color{blue}3}I$ maps $\\mathbf{u}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}3$ steps, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}3$   corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{blue}-3}$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y} \\xrightarrow{A-{\\color{black}5}I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-{\\color{black}5}I$ maps $\\mathbf{y}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $\\color{red}1$ step, \nthe vector $\\mathbf{y}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $\\color{red}1,$ not $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151963 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "79ffef9f-8be0-581f-b2f1-dc796d2cf94b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9057" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/f1a51fd3bae168f0b61e564cc421b2258c70351684c0480538b09ead5763aacd.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$\n\n- $\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v_n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_n \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\ldots \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{v}_{n-2},\\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\n- First, we compute $A-I{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/d07685a00b1e304d31fdf1b2c4067d8bf521023c448c2ccae8e918d269159ec6.png)\n\n- Next, we find the image of $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/ff5818e172da1edc5b12e07f16b232964cc9b051a22498949119f5ae536e3ef8.png)\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{w}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/f7450b8ba8ba29d97b413cb50d429cfb1a9aa534d5908e1917b426068cbb1bdf.png)\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $\\mathbf{x}$ under the action of $A-I{:}$\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/27c1a886c0b7cb4c4dfa427de1dcb44da5a98d70c24dd80a877d62f7ff9114e5.png)\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070419/assets/1a9cac337deaeaffeafb270281404e3a1917abcf85914fd8b737df8d89a3af01.png)\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $\\mathbf{v}$ to $\\mathbf{0}$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\qquad$ *$\\mathbf{v}$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a209982b-cb18-55e5-b09e-0d2d4f332fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148765} {:answer/id #uuid "fffc6e57-dec5-5b32-8e66-3233670c8659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148766} {:answer/id #uuid "169ecf63-4076-50c6-94cd-76cffb554498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148767} {:answer/id #uuid "266b3eba-6045-5104-9608-589d5bd1cf50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148768} {:answer/id #uuid "50041473-2324-553a-9a74-b20330263d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "169ecf63-4076-50c6-94cd-76cffb554498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148767} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9e410a-3054-5995-8499-24512b0280d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86e7e59c-5f60-5647-9c15-90e1d3a1ac43" :question/math-academy-id "q-109113" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366413 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9801fd32-7040-5043-b60d-0842803d6d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366415} {:answer/id #uuid "69444cfd-c2aa-5f41-ae72-763f6632cd17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366416} {:answer/id #uuid "d721cce9-8dab-5f3c-aa6c-2d42ae65e5f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366417} {:answer/id #uuid "db248ac1-d555-551e-bac9-48e7c8c55706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366418} {:answer/id #uuid "3467c7d2-ef7c-53b2-8660-6bf24818928c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9801fd32-7040-5043-b60d-0842803d6d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366415} :answer-field/id #uuid "34071159-f62f-5026-a6bc-1c873e7e40b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0da4560d-8d32-55a0-ba8a-cf38fa30a216" :question/math-academy-id "q-109236" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-I$ and $A+4I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-I=A-1I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d33d5ffd-146c-5ebe-94fe-1bae47eebf46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366422} {:answer/id #uuid "9165eb46-48ea-5bbc-b9b8-ded0d6e42ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366423} {:answer/id #uuid "400d592e-b461-5ae6-beaf-a4f4572abf50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366424} {:answer/id #uuid "cb72a4ce-cf48-5da6-98d5-fe2d85ec6258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366425} {:answer/id #uuid "d3077751-8be4-5b20-a54c-c8ac25a27d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d33d5ffd-146c-5ebe-94fe-1bae47eebf46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366422} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e8728ab-7b74-5e6a-8ffe-d874cbcc8dfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59eda5ae-a1dd-5e43-a828-e0cb5d1689f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-109244" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A-6I$ and $A+7I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-6I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-6I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+7I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+7I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ \ncorresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47799e92-9433-5784-b967-fa1bbec50a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366429} {:answer/id #uuid "a3879c73-31b3-51cf-b87d-96c30855bc5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366430} {:answer/id #uuid "27824fe1-abbe-567e-b818-8b2c25394f7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366431} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d58b16-f6ca-5024-bf83-e2772118c077" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366432} {:answer/id #uuid "407a00f2-7abc-5d37-ab3a-26ab0c14079f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "407a00f2-7abc-5d37-ab3a-26ab0c14079f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366433} :answer-field/id #uuid "8228f522-8db8-5971-9887-5be0b6762134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0d2f920-b57a-5668-bddf-d676b2af8a96" :question/math-academy-id "q-109273" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+8I$ and $A$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized  eigenvector of rank $1$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A$\n- $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+8I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nu\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+8I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+8I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is an generalized eigenvector of rank $3$  corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-8$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{\\,\\,\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector), not $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77ca64d1-e096-54db-999e-a24572fc0e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366436} {:answer/id #uuid "179b6a51-662a-51f4-b787-d9e02ef97352" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366437} {:answer/id #uuid "664a9247-bd4c-5ed7-8b2e-5641e671fa79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366438} {:answer/id #uuid "afb60dba-22ae-5e94-8116-c285cbad0616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366439} {:answer/id #uuid "3cfbec72-660f-5edb-94b5-c9ea3381f075" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "664a9247-bd4c-5ed7-8b2e-5641e671fa79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366438} :answer-field/id #uuid "abfeebf6-09ff-5fe9-8ec1-f185c175a74f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "95d7a2d5-8ecd-5919-bfa1-f13e328b9ad8" :question/math-academy-id "q-109431" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+20I$ and $A-15I$ on the nonzero vectors $v,w,x$ and $y$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =15$ of $A$\n- $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since \n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }w,\n$$\n\nand $w\\ne 0,$  the vector $v$ is  **not** a eigenvector corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$  of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nx\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A-15I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\n \ni.e. $A-15I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\nw\\overset{A+20I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+20I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-20$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "04926916-8e08-5401-a57b-3fd333741913" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors Using Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c24fd58d-7f6e-5813-baaf-fa55bd582a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366443} {:answer/id #uuid "a12aa751-a59a-5a5d-a1ab-8ed658359634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366444} {:answer/id #uuid "abd4c2c8-8cf4-5342-a3b4-986d6dac328b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366445} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa79757-e6f1-597f-91ed-1f1e42a916ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366446} {:answer/id #uuid "bba8155f-7354-591b-b13d-0497cae3d99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba8155f-7354-591b-b13d-0497cae3d99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366447} :answer-field/id #uuid "54197615-de76-5d3f-bc53-13274945e3a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a70bdc0-c7e2-5653-ac14-9e592e53f65a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109440" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0 \\\\ x\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven the action of the matrices $A+4I$ and $A+3I$ on the nonzero vectors $u,v,w,x,$ $y$ and $z$ as depicted above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n- $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$\n- $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since\n\n$$\nv\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $3.$\n\n- Statement II is  true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nx\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }y\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $x$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $x$ is a generalzed eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding  to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }z\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $2$ steps, \nthe vector $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ not $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eee89eb7-deb5-5561-bcef-87fe270ce52f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148783} {:answer/id #uuid "2501b1c2-807f-51b4-a2fd-eb1376cfd7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148784} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8df64b-4747-5783-90f4-8b0602f55568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148785} {:answer/id #uuid "554903b1-3664-5acc-ac17-b3ca5dc73f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148786} {:answer/id #uuid "6bae7c05-3161-59b5-97fb-9a3d9e245824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554903b1-3664-5acc-ac17-b3ca5dc73f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148786} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d7d4d84-f91a-5129-b785-67b83ec86582" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186148782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13d405ca-1969-5fbd-88a4-d12478e301e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109500" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$ \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7236b6b8-a62b-5c5d-864e-873e77ce0260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366450} {:answer/id #uuid "a03ca40e-3cc6-5775-a09b-41e12178885e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366451} {:answer/id #uuid "888c656c-bb67-5dcf-bff6-bd0cb830624d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366452} {:answer/id #uuid "d9edad67-c975-5b5e-b0ce-3af9f20ecc36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366453} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa68c11-8dc1-59ee-8b51-2bce5547eb21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a03ca40e-3cc6-5775-a09b-41e12178885e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366451} :answer-field/id #uuid "75ba1018-5456-57ab-8698-37cb9146cf76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46d5746c-58c4-521c-866a-554f81331255" :question/math-academy-id "q-109506" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine out statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-3I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfd7a7be-1262-5a4e-91a2-5aea60fe214d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366457} {:answer/id #uuid "7a132eca-ed35-595a-b6c9-966a824b7ff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366458} {:answer/id #uuid "7429ce49-90cb-5a8a-b247-e1ea2b3d42d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366459} {:answer/id #uuid "08885a00-8aaf-50b4-a8c7-d05c0bc248f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366460} {:answer/id #uuid "45c78bd1-f8ca-5d21-8729-9f7184c4981e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45c78bd1-f8ca-5d21-8729-9f7184c4981e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366461} :answer-field/id #uuid "5771d30c-94b4-5127-a6fc-5a0950fc1645" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48271141-348d-5274-bfac-677a8bcf2c89" :question/math-academy-id "q-109508" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A+4I:$\n\n$$\nA+4I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+4I:$\n\n$$\n(A+4I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+4I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b4f51ce-0352-5b68-884f-5b8cbc21a2f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366464} {:answer/id #uuid "aab3a7bc-0688-56ef-9937-04e5189c4e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366465} {:answer/id #uuid "4c27d54e-3efc-506b-b59d-8b7d4d12fa34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366466} {:answer/id #uuid "32a07663-fcc7-5f19-a959-c32862d20cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366467} {:answer/id #uuid "46aed99a-fff2-5252-9560-89a808220673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aab3a7bc-0688-56ef-9937-04e5189c4e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366465} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0da3b8-0788-5c27-9ed9-18bd4d3fe95d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2cd4d72e-ae2b-59a8-b4de-d6bc5e741c12" :question/math-academy-id "q-109517" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-10I:$\n\n$$\nA-10I=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-10I:$\n\n$$\n(A-10I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following map:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-10I\\,\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we see that $A-10I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $1$ step.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue  $\\lambda =10$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1ef3b68-9e10-51c3-9beb-f2939a3f00fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366471} {:answer/id #uuid "248dbec9-a155-5eb4-84fe-8a8605ddd727" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366472} {:answer/id #uuid "d6062c59-2796-53c3-8fab-cb135f88f5dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366473} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9a5b14-0144-54d0-9566-c15fc4550241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366474} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbfc3f7-0fc4-5e43-9f52-a4671a456800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbbfc3f7-0fc4-5e43-9f52-a4671a456800" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366475} :answer-field/id #uuid "338cd356-3e9d-5771-ab37-fbf2a93ecbfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dacc8a8a-5641-5275-8917-63d5b973c493" :question/math-academy-id "q-109524" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2c4dc3d-13a7-5c92-ab4d-0fad43f7152a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366478} {:answer/id #uuid "72aa8913-4bf0-50f6-9421-7afc468c343f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366479} {:answer/id #uuid "3b64f4c9-fce1-5a1a-8f6f-8c8d1d9f1b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of thee statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366480} {:answer/id #uuid "7a41224b-ce18-5300-be70-ba75f562f248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366481} {:answer/id #uuid "01b0c447-ca12-5294-97e8-9039976c20fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72aa8913-4bf0-50f6-9421-7afc468c343f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366479} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece8fc05-f50f-5c43-955c-29d3e258e525" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5399518b-8bef-5f85-8d7b-98dd47800c78" :question/math-academy-id "q-109525" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-I:$\n\n$$\nA-I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-I:$\n\n$$\n(A-I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26942055-c741-5908-a967-fa748dbe2950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366485} {:answer/id #uuid "7a59d0a2-e02b-5e84-a720-1835618f18bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366486} {:answer/id #uuid "c1da86bb-a22b-56ff-86ee-e75c632b1715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366487} {:answer/id #uuid "48ed199e-0a7e-5598-82ac-ba428f1887af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366488} {:answer/id #uuid "972951e5-1721-551f-939a-b6bc08595889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26942055-c741-5908-a967-fa748dbe2950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366485} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b103ac2-a21d-52cc-8481-ddc38d102a87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7659191f-b5f5-5d8e-9baa-bdc8ca17c847" :question/math-academy-id "q-109585" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+2I:$\n\n$$\nA+2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ad156d9-f527-5b65-a3b2-7ebccc8e56e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366492} {:answer/id #uuid "b64de4d3-36c0-5853-a0ca-1a93378ab1a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366493} {:answer/id #uuid "def75f8e-0144-573e-933e-a3a2f190a060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366494} {:answer/id #uuid "f220a480-64a3-5611-abb9-af01dbc5ebcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366495} {:answer/id #uuid "2aef68f3-57bb-566a-8530-9b7997deeade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def75f8e-0144-573e-933e-a3a2f190a060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366494} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ac12a9b-6432-587a-82f8-66873b133e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4764bc2d-97dc-5b64-a376-d967b94c6c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-109626" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+2I:$\n\n$$\nA+2I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)v=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)w=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)x=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -9 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "76e60bc4-ae61-5c39-ad80-14e0cdab14da" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f06f515-71f0-54a6-a682-d082e002cbe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366499} {:answer/id #uuid "30fefb7d-7388-54cf-99dc-32fff560343a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366500} {:answer/id #uuid "71f14a96-d77a-59bb-881f-07c81aabe125" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366501} {:answer/id #uuid "96d035d3-88c0-5577-97b4-f032a4184a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366502} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc6ad87-8185-5275-8e54-6149c40c6b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f06f515-71f0-54a6-a682-d082e002cbe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366499} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bad65cb-acfb-5b19-95cd-02c1962f59b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f1d809eb-c7d6-58f4-a2f0-697f8d8877e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-109632" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n- $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}]}
